Z||000018||Sage||06-08-2000||08:43 AM||||George<P>That is some nice fiberglass work.  It is hard to explain to people how mold making is done.  But suffice it to say,(and I am sure I will be corrected if wrong)that mold making costs approx. 10x the amount that a part costs to produce.<P>In other words, if you can produce a hood for $150.00,(that's materials only, no labor, no profit and that is a totaly fictictious figure for demonstrative purposes) you can plan on spending around $1,500.00 to produce a good quality mold.  That's including the plug, which you already have, using good materials including gel-coat, which in my opinion is a must for a good mold.<P>Then keep in mind that this mold is not indestructable, that you may get several hundred pulls from it, if you made it right!  Then it will have to be refurbed, or replaced.<P>The mold it in yourself kit would be much more practical to procuce, but fewer people would be interested, or at least able to install it  with the desired results.<P>Have toyed with the idea of producing a hood with the L-88 scoop on it, as I already have the mold for the scoop, and some extra hoods, but for the reasons stated above and lack of a market, Have not done it.  The L-88 looks sweet on the Fiero hood, and it would be functional.  $$$$$  both ways is the stopper!<P>Just my.002...everybody have a good on one!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 06-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||TOM||06-08-2000||11:07 AM||||being in the business I could make a good quality mold that will make a hundred parts before it need any attention. I know I have done it. For around $250 materials and my time. It only takes about 2 hours. But thats just me. I know alot of mold makers that charge up the wazoo because it is a specialized art. ||||reg||1
